Code derived from QualCoder
===========================

qualcoder-mcp works on QualCoder's own project files
(https://github.com/ccbogel/QualCoder). The routines listed below are
copied or transcribed from QualCoder's source, so that the numbers,
matches and offsets this server produces are exactly the ones QualCoder
produces for the same data. Everything else in qualcoder-mcp is written
for this project.

QualCoder is copyright Colin Curtain and contributors (its source file
headers name the authors as Colin Curtain, Kai Dröge, Justin
Missaghieh--Poncet and Lorenzo Salomón), and is licensed under the GNU
Lesser General Public License, version 3 or (at your option) any later
version (LGPL-3.0-or-later). The routines below remain under that
licence.

QualCoder file and line references are to QualCoder's master branch at
commit 9bddf17f86aa457aff72c3a38dbc10a37f296ad2, under src/qualcoder/,
unless a reference is marked 3.8.2 (QualCoder's 3.8.2 release tag).

1. QualCoder's "Kappa" value
   Here: src/qualcoder_mcp/coder_comparison.py, function kappa_qualcoder.
   From: reports.py, lines 1140-1151 (the same expression appears in
   report_compare_coder_file.py, lines 818-830).
   Why: so the value reported as kappa_qualcoder is the number QualCoder's
   Coder comparison report displays, computed by the same expressions in
   the same order and therefore identical to the last floating-point
   digit.

2. QualCoder's coder comparison counting
   Here: src/qualcoder_mcp/coder_comparison.py, function
   qualcoder_report_values.
   From: reports.py, lines 1061-1108.
   Why: so a comparison result can state exactly what QualCoder's report
   would show for the same data where QualCoder counts differently from
   this server (one coder's overlapping segments of the same code).

3. QualCoder's coding editor span walk
   Here: src/qualcoder_mcp/pseudonymise.py, method SpanMapper._parity
   (the overlap policy qualcoder_edit_parity).
   From: code_text.py, lines 5893-5927 (apply_insert and apply_delete),
   driven by the running offset of lines 5933-5950.
   Why: so that under this policy a coded span moves, shrinks or is
   deleted exactly as QualCoder's own editor would move, shrink or delete
   it when a name is deleted and its pseudonym typed in its place.

4. QualCoder's whole-word replacement pattern
   Here: src/qualcoder_mcp/pseudonymise.py, the replacement pattern built
   in Compiled.__init__.
   From: manage_files.py, line 3348.
   Why: so a name is replaced at exactly the positions QualCoder's own
   file import replaces it: a word boundary on both sides, and the name
   escaped so that it is never read as a regular expression.

5. Test oracles: QualCoder's replacement patterns
   Here: tests/test_v012_pseudonymise_engine.py, functions
   master_boundary_spans and legacy_boundary_spans.
   From: manage_files.py, line 3348 (master_boundary_spans); 3.8.2
   manage_files.py, lines 2035-2040, and import_survey.py, lines 134-140
   (legacy_boundary_spans, the older unescaped form).
   Why: the tests compare this server's matches with QualCoder's at every
   position, and pin the documented differences from the older form.

6. Test oracle: QualCoder's coding editor span walk
   Here: tests/test_v012_pseudonymise_engine.py, class MasterEditWalk.
   From: code_text.py, lines 5893-5927, driven by the running offset of
   lines 5933-5950.
   Why: a second copy kept in QualCoder's own shape (every row walked per
   edit, where item 3 walks every edit per row), so that the tests check
   item 3 against QualCoder's walk rather than against itself.

7. Test oracle: the coder comparison reference
   Here: tests/test_v012_compare_coders.py, class TestReferenceEquivalence.
   From: no QualCoder code of its own. It runs items 1 and 2 as the
   reference, and the expected values in class TestParityPins are the
   values QualCoder's report displays for the same data (reports.py,
   lines 1049-1152).
   Why: to check that this server's statistics equal QualCoder's wherever
   the two ways of counting agree.

8. QualCoder's colour palette
   Here: src/qualcoder_mcp/database.py, the list QUALCODER_COLORS.
   From: color_selector.py, lines 52-65 (the list colors, 120 colours).
   Why: so a code created by this server takes its colour from the same
   palette as a code created in QualCoder, and a colour the AI supplies is
   snapped onto that palette.
